Below are two self‑contained files you can drop into your NixOS/Home‑Manager setup:

* **`flake.nix`** – a tiny flake that pulls in the required packages, enables the Emacs distribution you already use, configures **notmuch** (via Home‑Manager’s `programs.notmuch` module), sets up a **plocate** update timer, and makes the seforim directory available as a maildir.
* **`seforim.el`** – the Emacs‑Lisp snippet (you can also keep it as an Org‑mode block and tangle it) that gives you the *plocate*‑backed file‑path search, the *notmuch*‑backed full‑text search, the outline helper, the dired‑sidebar integration and the `C‑c S` hydra you asked for.

You can keep the files wherever you like; the example assumes they live in `~/.config/seforim/flake.nix` and `~/.config/seforim/seforim.el`.
After you have the flake, run `home-manager switch -f ~/.config/seforim/flake.nix` (or add the flake as an input to your existing flake and import the home configuration) and the whole stack will be ready.

---

## 1️⃣ `flake.nix`

```nix
{
  description = "Home‑Manager configuration for the Seforim library";

  inputs = {
    nixpkgs.url = "github:nixos/nixpkgs/nixos-unstable";
    home-manager.url = "github:nix-community/home-manager";
    home-manager.inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
  };

  outputs = { self, nixpkgs, home-manager }:
    let
      system = "x86_64-linux";
      pkgs   = import nixpkgs { inherit system; };
    in
    {
      homeConfigurations."shaul@myhost" = home-manager.lib.homeManagerConfiguration {
        pkgs = pkgs;
        modules = [
          # -------------------------------------------------
          # 1. Basic Emacs setup (keep your existing config)
          # -------------------------------------------------
          { programs.emacs.enable = true;
            # Use your own Emacs config – replace the path if you keep it elsewhere.
            programs.emacs.extraPackages = epkgs: with epkgs; [
              vertico
              consult
              orderless
              marginalia              hydra
              dired-sidebar
              all-the-icons
              notmuch   # Emacs interface to notmuch
            ];
            # If you keep a separate init.el, you can load it here:
            programs.emacs.init = ''
              (load-file "~/.config/seforim/seforim.el")
            '';
          }

          # -------------------------------------------------
          # 2. notmuch – maildir pointing at your seforim tree
          # -------------------------------------------------
          { programs.notmuch.enable = true;
            programs.notmuch.maildir.seforim = "/home/shaul/Documents/seforim";
            # Ignore the usual VCS/backup/binary cruft – feel free to extend.
            programs.notmuch.ignore = [
              ".git"
              ".gitignore"
              ".rgignore"
              "*~"
              "#*#"
              "*.org~"
              "*.tmp"
              "*.bak"
              "*.pdf"
              "*.epub"
              "*.mobi"
            ];
            # Optional: show the seforim tag in the summary line
            programs.notmuch.notmuchrc.extraConfig = ''
              [new]
                # tag incoming mail with the maildir name (seforim)
                # (not strictly needed for plain‑text files, but harmless)
                tags = seforim
            '';
          }

          # -------------------------------------------------
          # 3. plocate – filename index (super‑fast path search)
          # -------------------------------------------------
          { programs.plocate.enable = true;
            # Automatically keep the plocate db up‑to‑date (once a day)
            programs.plocate.enableAutoUpdate = true;
          }

          # -------------------------------------------------          # 4. Systemd user timer to keep notmuch fresh
          # -------------------------------------------------
          { services.user.systemd.timers."notmuch-seforim.timer" = {
                description = "Daily notmuch index update for seforim library";
                wantedBy = [ "timers.target" ];
                partOf = [ "notmuch-seforim.service" ];
                timerConfig.OnCalendar = "daily";
                timerConfig.Persistent = true;
              };
            services.user.systemd.services."notmuch-seforim.service" = {
                description = "Run notmuch new on the seforim maildir";
                serviceConfig.Type = "oneshot";
                serviceConfig.ExecStart = "${pkgs.notmuch}/bin/notmuch new";
              };
          }

          # -------------------------------------------------
          # 5. Make sure the seforim directory exists (optional safety net)
          # -------------------------------------------------
          { home.file."/home/shaul/Documents/seforim".source = ./.;
            home.file."/home/shaul/Documents/seforim".type = "symlink";
            # If you already have the directory elsewhere, you can just
            # comment this out – the timer and notmuch will work as long as
            # the path exists.
          }
        ];
      };
    };
}
```

### What the flake does

| Section | Purpose |
|--------|---------|
| **Emacs** | Installs Emacs with the exact packages you already use (`vertico`, `consult`, `orderless`, `marginalia`, `hydra`, `dired-sidebar`, `all-the-icons`, `notmuch`). Your personal `init.el` (or any other loader) is invoked via `programs.emacs.init`. |
| **notmuch** | Declares a maildir called `seforim` that points at `~/Documents/seforim`. All files under that tree are automatically tagged with `seforim`. The `ignore` list mirrors a typical `.rgignore` so wasted work is avoided. |
| **plocate** | Installs `plocate` and enables its daily auto‑update timer (so `locate`/`plocate` stays fresh). |
| **notmuch timer** | A `systemd --user` timer that runs `notmuch new` once a day (adjust `OnCalendar` if you prefer a different cadence). The first run builds the Xapian index; later runs are incremental. |
| **Directory safety** | (optional) creates a symlink at `~/Documents/seforim` pointing to the flake directory – replace or delete this if you already keep the library elsewhere. |

> **Tip:** If you already have a Home‑Manager configuration, you can simply copy the relevant blocks (`programs.emacs`, `programs.notmuch`, `programs.plocate`, the `services.user.systemd.*` timer/service, and the optional home.file entry) into your existing `home.nix` or flake module.

